# Line endings, decided once.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every text file
# to CRLF on checkout. For most of a repository that is harmless. For the scripts
# in sh/ it is fatal, and fatal in a way that reads as a missing file:
#
#   $ sh/pull
#   bash: sh/pull: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# `env` is plainly there. What is missing is `bash`, because the shebang line ends
# `bash\r` and the carriage return is part of the name being looked up. Somebody
# hitting that on a fresh clone has no reason to suspect line endings, and every
# reason to suspect the script.
#
# So the whole tree is LF, in the repository and in the working copy, on every
# platform. Nothing here is read by a Windows-native tool that needs CRLF: the Go
# toolchain does not care, Git Bash wants LF, and every editor anybody uses on this
# has handled LF for a decade.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, so nothing tries to normalise them. The build output is ignored rather
# than committed, but a checkout can still carry a fixture or an image one day, and
# a normalised PNG is a corrupt PNG.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
